---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Routes from "../../components/Routes.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";
import { textChoices } from "../../lib/source.ts";

const CG = "codegen/src/iki_codegen/models.py";
const GM = "server/src/iki_server/graph/models.py";
const GQ = "server/src/iki_server/graph/queries.py";
const API = "server/src/iki_server/api/entities.py";
const edgeKinds = textChoices(GM, "EdgeKind");

const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: "A reference is a foreign key, and an edge in the graph" },
  { id: "how", label: "How references are stored and read" },
  { id: "graph", label: "The graph of what depends on what" },
  { id: "rules", label: "The rules in full" },
  { id: "ref", label: "Routes, files and decisions" },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title="A reference is a foreign key, and an edge in the graph">
    <p>
      A field of <code>type: ref</code> names another record, of one type, of several, or of an
      abstract type. It's a real foreign key in the type's table; a list of references is a table of
      its own, one row per item. A reference to a type with an identity names the registry entry, not
      a project's record. Reading a record gives each reference's ID in its values, and the referred
      records the reader can read, by ID; one they can't read shows as <code>unreadable</code>.
      Triggers keep an edge per reference in <code>iki_graph_edge</code>, beside the edges runs and
      the amounts ledger make, so lineage and impact walk one graph (<Adr n={[115, 123, 136]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title="How references are stored and read">
    <p>
      <strong>Declared.</strong> <code>to</code> names the type a reference may name, or several
      (<Adr n="0136" />). <code>list</code> with <code>items: {"{type: ref}"}</code> is a list of
      them, with optional <code>min_items</code> and <code>max_items</code>; a list of rows can hold
      references in its row fields (<Adr n="0137" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Stored.</strong> The code generator makes a single reference a <code>ForeignKey</code>{" "}
      with <code>PROTECT</code>, so a referred record can't be deleted from under it (<Path
        p={CG}
        sym="def _ref_target("
      />). What it points to:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>one concrete type: that type's table of records;</li>
      <li>a type with an identity: its entries' table (<code>&lt;table&gt;__entry</code>);</li>
      <li>an abstract type, several types or any entity: <code>iki_entity</code>, with triggers that refuse a write naming an entity of another type, whatever makes it (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/targets.py" />).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A list of references is a table <code>&lt;table&gt;__&lt;field&gt;</code> keyed on (entity,
      position), each <code>item</code> its own foreign key, with a CHECK for <code>max_items</code>.
      In the history table a reference is a bare UUID, with no foreign key, so history survives
      whatever happens to what it pointed to (<PageLink to="Types and tables" />).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Read.</strong> A record's <code>values</code> carry each reference's ID, and <code
        >refs</code
      > carries, by ID, each referred record the reader can read: its HRID, title, type, and whether
      it's archived or purged. A reference to an entry is titled by the reading project's own record
      of it where it has one, as its link opens that record (<Path p={API} sym="def refs_in(" />). A
      reference to something the reader can't read is the string <code>unreadable</code> instead of
      an ID: <PageLink to="Unreadable references" /> covers why and where. A run pins the references
      its inputs hold at launch: <PageLink to="Pinning and provenance" />.
    </p>

    <h3 id="graph">The graph of what depends on what</h3>
    <p>
      <code>iki_graph_edge</code> holds one row per dependency: <code>node</code> depends on <code
        >on</code
      >, for a reason that's its <code>kind</code>. A reference edge also says which field, which
      column or list table holds it and, for a list, its place; a run's edge names the run and the
      revision it read (<Path p={GM} sym="class Edge(" />).
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Label</th><th>Note in the code</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {edgeKinds.map((k) => <tr><td><code>{k.value}</code></td><td>{k.label}</td><td>{k.note}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Statement-level triggers, which <code>sync_plugins</code> installs on every table holding
      references and every list of them, keep reference edges in step with the columns, so whatever
      writes a reference (a person, a run, a bulk import, a bundle or SQL) writes its edge, and
      clearing it takes the edge away (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/graph/edges.py" />). A
      reference edge is what the field holds now; run and file edges are history. The functions are
      the migrator's (<code>SECURITY DEFINER</code>) and the app's role can only read the table.
      The aliquot, transfer and used edges come from the amounts ledger: <PageLink to="Amounts and the ledger" />.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>upstream(X)</code> and <code>downstream(X)</code> are recursive CTEs over the edges, as
      far as asked (10 by default, 30 at most), through the kinds asked for; a record's edge to its
      entry is always followed. Each result has its <code>path</code> back to X, the first way the walk
      found it (<Path p={GQ} sym="def walk(" />). They're on every surface: the web app's Lineage page
      (a tree, a list or a graph), search conditions (<code>downstream:TUB-000001</code>), the API and
      its CSVs, the MCP tools <code>get_lineage</code> and <code>get_impact</code>, and triggers that
      give a run what's related to the record that fired it (<PageLink to="Workflows and triggers" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title="The rules in full">
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="A walk shows only what you can read" open>
        <p>
          An edge is the data of the entity that states it: a reference is its holder's, a run's edge
          is the made entity's provenance. Downstream, the walk only reaches entities the caller can
          read; what refers to X from a project they can't read isn't named, counted or walked
          through. Upstream, a reference they can see leading to an entity they can't is counted in{" "}
          <code>hidden</code>, never named, and the walk stops there. Record edges go only to records
          they can read, so how many projects hold an entry never shows (<Adr n="0123" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Bounded work">
        <p>
          Each node is walked once per depth it's reached at, so diamonds and cycles cost no more than
          the nodes times the depth, and each walk runs within search's time limit, answering <code
            >search_too_slow</code
          > past it. <code>GET …/graph</code>, for a picture, is capped at 200 entities and 500 edges
          (<Path p={GQ} sym="def graph(" />). Every literal in a walk's SQL is checked to be a UUID, a
          name or a number, so nothing a caller sends is in it.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Moving a reference to a replacing entry">
        <p>
          When a registrar supersedes an entry and a project moves its record to the replacement, the
          project's own references to the old entry are re-pointed, each a revision with the reason
          (<PageLink to="Identity and HRIDs" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title="Routes, files and decisions">
    <Routes tags={["graph"]} />
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Where</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/graph" />: edges and walks</li>
          <li><Path p="server/src/iki_server/entities/targets.py" />: what a reference may name</li>
          <li><Path p={CG} />: reference columns and list tables</li>
          <li><Path p="web/app/src/lineage.ts" />: the Lineage page's queries and layout</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0115" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0123" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0136" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0137" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
